-- title:
--   Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation along the Hecke tower, one-leg form
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are primes with $\bar r\neq r$ (`hrr`) and $N$ is a nonzero natural number with $r\nmid N$ (`hrN`), $\bar r\nmid N$ (`hrbarN`) and $N$ squarefree (`hN`).
--
--   **Coefficients.** $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi\in\mathcal O$ is irreducible (`hπ`), $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Next, `Onr` is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal O$-algebra carrying an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism `Fr`, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), with that ideal maximal (`hOnr_max`), such that every element satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over `Onr` has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and `Fr` induces $x\mapsto x^{r}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hFr`); thus `Onr` plays the role of the completion of the maximal unramified extension. Finally `vdet` is a homomorphism $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $\mathrm{vdet}(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g=u\,\pi^{n}$ for some $u\in\mathcal O^{\times}$, i.e. `vdet` is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   **Indefinite side and moduli.** The hypothesis `hB` says that $a>0$ or $b>0$ and that the finite places of $\mathbb Q$ at which $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is a division algebra are exactly those containing $r$ or $\bar r$; $\Lambda$ is a maximal order (`hΛ`) containing the image of $\mathbb Z$ (`hΛℤ`). The triple $(\mathcal X,f,\mathrm{pt})$, with $f:\mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure (`h𝒳`: invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometric points over algebraically closed fields, and the universal property). An auxiliary level $n$ satisfies $3\le n$ (`hn`), $r\nmid n$ (`hrn`), $\bar r\nmid n$ (`hrbarn`) and $\gcd(n,N)=1$ (`hnN`), and $(M,f_M,\mathrm{ptF})$ is a fine moduli space for such curves equipped with a full level-$n$ structure (`hM`). A group $G$ acts through $\rho:G\to\operatorname{Aut}M$ with labels $\chi:G\to\Lambda$, and `hρ` asserts that this is a level-twist action (each $\rho(h)$ lies over the base, it realises the $\chi(h)$-twist of full level structures, and the labels are multiplicative and bijective modulo $n$). A morphism $p:M\to\mathcal X$ lies over the base (`hp`), is $G$-invariant (`hρp`) and carries fine moduli points to coarse ones (`hp_pt`). For every prime $\ell\notin\{r,\bar r\}$ there is a coarse moduli space $(\mathcal Y_\ell,g_\ell,\mathrm{ptT}_\ell)$ for curves with an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup (`h𝒴`), together with degeneracy maps $d_0(\ell),d_1(\ell):\mathcal Y_\ell\to\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) which on moduli points forget the extra level (`hd₀`) respectively pass to the quotient by a level-$\ell$ isogeny (`hd₁`). Two endomorphisms $a_r,a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ (`harf`, `harbarf`) are given on moduli points by the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite side.** The hypothesis `hdef` says $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is a division algebra exactly at the place containing $\bar r$; $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order (`hΛ₁`), $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (`hR₁`) contained in $\Lambda_1$ (`hRΛ₁`); $n_1$ is an adelic unit in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ (`hn₁`) for which $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1)$ is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); $\iota_0$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $M_2(K_0)$ (`hι₀`); $v$ is the height-one prime containing $r$ (`hv`). The group $\Gamma_t$ consists precisely of the units lying in the local unit boxes of $R_1$ at all places other than $v$ (`hΓt`). Families $s_\ell$ of global units and $s\!f_\ell$ of adelic units satisfy `hs` (four clauses: $s\!f_\ell$ agrees with $s_\ell$ away from $r$, equals $1$ at $r$, the diagonal idele of $\ell$ times $s\!f_\ell^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set of $(\Lambda_1,\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1))$ at $\ell$ if $\ell\mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1)$ at $\ell$ otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell)=\ell$), and $\Gamma_{t,\ell}=\Gamma_t\cap s_\ell\Gamma_t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). A unit $\bar w$ has reduced norm $\bar r$ and normalises $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   **Base curve in characteristic $r$ and its $\Gamma_t$-action.** A map $\mathrm{coord}:\Lambda\to\mathbb Z_{p^2}(r)\times\mathbb Z_{p^2}(r)$ is an order coordinate system (`hcoord`: additive, unital, multiplicative with the Frobenius-twisted rule, injective, with dense image and the prescribed trace identity). Over $Onr/(\pi)$ there are a fake elliptic curve $A_0$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$, formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of $A_0$ in two variables, families $e,e'$ of endomorphisms of $A_0$ indexed by $\Gamma_t$ with $e\gamma$ over the base (`he`) and degrees recorded by $\mathrm{deg}$; $X_0$ has height $4$ (`hX₀`) and $A_0$ is a formal module via $\mathrm{coord}$, $X_0$, $\theta_0$ (`hA₀`). The clauses `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here) state: each $(e\gamma,e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level-$N$ structure; $\gamma\mapsto e\gamma$ is multiplicative up to multiplication by powers of $r$; scalars $c\in\mathbb Z$ act by multiplication by $c$ up to powers of $r$; conversely, if $e\gamma$ becomes a multiplication then $\gamma$ is a rational scalar; for every injective ring map $E_0$ of the centraliser of the $X_0$-action together with the uniformiser endomorphism into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e\gamma$ is induced on the formal group by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0\varepsilon=r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; full level-$n$ structures on $A_0$ acquire $\Lambda$-valued labels $\mathrm{lab}$ for the $\Gamma_t$-action, multiplicative and scalar-compatible modulo $n$; and every level-preserving isogeny pair of $r$-power degree of a fake elliptic curve pulled back from $A_0$ over an algebraically closed field comes, up to powers of $r$, from some $e\gamma$.
--
--   **Endomorphisms from orders.** An order $R_2\le R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) with $r$-power denominators in $R_1$ (`hR₂r`) is realised by endomorphisms $\hat e$ of $A_0$ over the base (`hê`); the clauses `hE5a`, `hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3`, `hE5c`, `hE5d`, `hE5e`, `hE5g` (summarised here) require that $\hat e$ be additive on points, commute with the $\Lambda$-action, preserve the level structure, be unital and multiplicative, send integers to the corresponding multiplications, satisfy $\hat e(\bar x)\hat e(x)=\mathrm{nrd}(x)$, agree with $e\gamma$ on $r$-power multiples of $\gamma$, admit — for a single $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ — the joint matrix description of both the $\Gamma_t$- and the $R_2$-action through $\iota_0$ up to powers of $r$, and that $R_2$ be stable under conjugation. A twin maximal order $\Lambda_{1}^{s}$ (`hΛ₁s`) contains $R_1$ (`hR₁Λ₁s`) with $\Lambda_1\cap\Lambda_1^{s}=R_1$ (`htwin`), and an order $R_2'\le\Lambda_1^{s}$ (`hR₂'`, `hR₂'o`) with $r$-power denominators in $\Lambda_1^{s}$ (`hR₂'r`) contains $R_2$ (`hR₂R₂'`) and is realised by endomorphisms $\hat e'$ over the base (`hê'`); the clauses `hE6a`, `hE6b1`, `hE6b2`, `hE6b3`, `hE6c`, `hE6d`, `hE6e1`, `hE6e2`, `hE6f` (summarised here) require the analogous additivity, $\Lambda$-equivariance, unitality, multiplicativity, integrality, norm identity, agreement with $\hat e$ on $R_2$, stability under conjugation, injectivity of $z\mapsto\hat e'z$, and that an element killing the $q$-torsion for a prime $q\neq r$ become divisible by $q$ in $\Lambda_1^{s}$ after multiplication by a power of $r$.
--
--   **Drinfeld side.** There are a ring map $\iota:\mathbb Z_{p^2}(r)\to Onr$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special relative to the reduction of $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`), a moduli package $MD$ for $r$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`), and a family $\eta$ sending rigidified objects to $MD$-points, with `hη` (three clauses: $\eta$ identifies two admissible rigidified objects exactly when they are isomorphic, is natural in the base ring, and is surjective after passage to a Zariski cover). An injective ring map $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action and its uniformiser endomorphism into $M_2(K_0)$ satisfies `hE₀` (injectivity, and commensurability of the image with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ up to a fixed power $r^{m}$). The family $eD$, with `hnatD` (naturality), `hbijD` (bijectivity), `hfstD` (the first component is the structure map $\psi$), `hGLD` (the $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action on $MD$-points corresponds to the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` on the product functor), `hPiD` and `hPiexD` ($\pi$-translation of $MD$-points corresponds to a Frobenius twist of the first factor, and always exists), identifies the functor of $G$-points of $MD$ with the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$ and Drinfeld's functor $\Omega$ of Deligne data over $K_0$ with parameter $\pi$. Further data: a residue map $\kappa$ from $Onr/(\pi)$ to $Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), an integer $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ which is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to the $\kappa$-base change of $X_0$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`), and a full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner datum at $\bar r$ over the base.** A curve $A_{0,w}$, maps $a_w,a_w'$ exhibiting it as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ at $\bar r$ (`hALw`, with `haw`, `haw'`), an isogeny pair $b_w,b_w'$ of degree $r^{k_w}$ from $A_{0,w}$ to $A_0$ (`hBSw`, with `hbw`, `habw`) preserving the level (`hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw`, which asserts that a single conjugating $g$ matches both the $\Gamma_t$-action and the composite $a_w$ followed by $b_w$ with $\iota_0(\bar w)$ up to powers of $r$.
--
--   **The rigidification-to-$G$-points map.** For every Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent and every $\psi:Onr\to B$, the map $\Xi$ sends a pair consisting of a fake elliptic curve over $B$ and a rigidification of it along $A_0$ to a $G$-point of $MD$; `hΞleg` says that over $B$ with no nontrivial idempotents the resulting structure map is a Frobenius twist of $\psi$, `hΞnat` that $\Xi$ is compatible with pullback, `hΞiso` that it is unchanged under isomorphisms of rigidified curves matching the rigidification data up to powers of $r$, and `hΞdef` gives its explicit description: for a curve with a formal module structure there are $j\le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ with $t.X=X$, transporting the rigidification through $\theta_0,\kappa,\beta_0$, admissible for the $(-j)$-Frobenius twist of $\psi$, and $\Xi$ is computed by $\eta$ applied to $t$.
--
--   **Equivariance.** For a fixed $g_0\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$: `heqΓ` states that translating a rigidification by $e\gamma$, $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$, corresponds on $G$-points to the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$ for some integer $c$; `heqW` states the same for the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ and $\iota_0(\bar w)$ (over $B$ with no nontrivial idempotents and with $\bar r$ invertible); `heqS` states it for level-$\ell$ isogenies and $\iota_0(\ell\,s^{-1})$, under data $n_1$, $s$, $s\!f$ satisfying the same local conditions as above, level-$\ell$ isogeny maps $a_s,a_s'$ and an $r$-power isogeny pair $b_s,b_s'$ preserving the level, and a joint matrix normalisation `hJOINTs` involving $\iota_0(\bar s)$.
--
--   **The uniformisation at level $n$.** A group homomorphism $\theta_t:\Gamma_t\to G$ is given, together with $\Theta_f$, assigning to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent a map from triples $((\psi,P),h)$, where $\psi:Onr\to B$, $P$ is a Deligne datum over $B$ and $h\in G$, to the $B$-points of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. Its axioms are `hnat` (naturality in $B$), `hG` (right $G$-equivariance through $\rho$), `hi'` (invariance under the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$, $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$, combined with left translation by $\theta_t\gamma$), `het` (unique lifting along a surjection $B\to B_0$ whose kernel has vanishing pairwise products) and `hfib` (the exact criterion, over algebraically closed fields, for two triples to have the same image: a $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ with $g'=\theta_t\gamma\cdot g$, with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, and with $\psi'$ agreeing with the $(-\mathrm{vdet}\,\iota_0(\gamma))$-Frobenius twist of $\psi$ on all elements fixed by the Frobenius powers attached to the scalar elements of $\ker\theta_t$). Finally the clauses `hC0e`, `hC0u`, `hC1e`, `hC1u`, `hC1p`, `hC2`, `hC2c`, `hC3`, `hC4`, `hC4pi`, `hC5` (summarised here) relate $\Xi$, $eD$, $g_0$ and $\Theta_f$: existence and uniqueness of the normalised level-$n$ transport of $P_0$ along a rigidification; existence, uniqueness up to isomorphism of rigidified curves, and pullback of pairs $(E,\varrho)$ whose $\Xi$-leg is the $g_0$-twist of $\psi$ and whose $eD$-image is carried by $g_0^{-1}$ to $(\psi,P)$; the identification of $\Theta_f((\psi,P),1)$ with the fine moduli point of $(E,P_n)$ and of its image in $\mathcal X$ with the coarse point of $E$; the effect of the $\Gamma_t$-action and of a general $h$ with an `IsActBy` relation on level-$n$ and extra level-$\ell$ structures; the correspondence between $\pi$-translation and the Frobenius twist; and, in the one-leg form used here, the clause `hC5`, in which the two rigidifications compared over an algebraically closed field lie over one and the same $\psi$, and an isomorphism of the underlying curves is matched with some $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ acting by the twisted action on $(\psi,P)$, with the prescribed effect on level-$n$ structures (through $\chi(\theta_t\gamma)$) and on extra level-$\ell$ structures.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exists a family $\Theta_T$ assigning to each prime $\ell\notin\{r,\bar r\}$ and each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent a map from pairs $(\psi,P)$, $\psi:Onr\to_{\mathcal O} B$ and $P$ a Deligne datum over $B$, to the $B$-points of $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, such that the following six statements hold.
--
--   First, each $\Theta_T(\ell)$ is natural: for every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\varphi:B\to B'$ between such algebras, $\Theta_T(\ell)$ of the image of $(\psi,P)$ equals the image under $\varphi$ of $\Theta_T(\ell)(\psi,P)$.
--
--   Second, each $\Theta_T(\ell)$ is invariant under the twisted action of $\Gamma_{t,\ell}$: if $\gamma\in\Gamma_{t,\ell}$ and $x,x'$ are related by `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for the matrix $\iota_0(\gamma)$ — that is, the structure map of $x'$ is the $(-\mathrm{vdet}\,\iota_0(\gamma))$-Frobenius twist of that of $x$ and the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$ — then $\Theta_T(\ell)x'=\Theta_T(\ell)x$.
--
--   Third, over geometric points the fibres are exactly the $\Gamma_{t,\ell}$-orbits: for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent, every $\psi:Onr\to_{\mathcal O}k$ and all Deligne data $P,P'$ over $k$, one has $\Theta_T(\ell)(\psi,P)=\Theta_T(\ell)(\psi,P')$ if and only if there is $\gamma\in\Gamma_{t,\ell}$ with $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$.
--
--   Fourth, the family lifts to fine level: for every $\ell$ and every datum consisting of a scheme $M_\ell$ with $f_{M_\ell}$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and moduli points $\mathrm{ptF}_\ell$ making it a fine moduli space for curves with full level $n$ and an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup (`hMℓ`), a map $\pi_\ell:M_\ell\to M$ over the base (`hπℓf`) which forgets the extra level on moduli points (`hπℓ`), a map $p_\ell:M_\ell\to\mathcal Y_\ell$ over the base (`hpℓg`) inducing the coarse points with extra level (`hpℓ`), and an action $\rho_\ell:G\to\operatorname{Aut}M_\ell$ over the base (`hρℓf`) intertwining $\pi_\ell$ with $\rho$ (`hρℓπ`), fixing $p_\ell$ (`hρℓp`) and realising the $\chi$-twist of full level structures compatibly with the extra level (`hρℓtw`), there exists a map $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ from triples $((\psi,P),h)$ over such $B$ to the $B$-points of $M_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with all of: naturality in $B$; the $G$-equivariance $(\rho_\ell h)_*\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,gh)=\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,g)$; the invariance $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x',\theta_t\gamma\cdot g)=\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,g)$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ also lies in $\Gamma_{t,\ell}$ and $x,x'$ are related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$; the unique lifting property along every surjection $p:B\to B_0$ of $\mathcal O$-algebras with $\pi$ nilpotent in both and with the product of any two elements of $\ker p$ zero, namely that each $B$-point of $M_\ell$ reducing to $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ of a given triple over $B_0$ is $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ of a unique lift of that triple; the fibre criterion over algebraically closed $k$, that $\Theta_{f,\ell}((\psi,P),g)=\Theta_{f,\ell}((\psi',P'),g')$ if and only if there is $\gamma\in\Gamma_t$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_{t,\ell}$, $g'=\theta_t\gamma\cdot g$, $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, and $\psi'$ agreeing with the $(-\mathrm{vdet}\,\iota_0(\gamma))$-Frobenius twist of $\psi$ on every $y\in Onr$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{\mathrm{vdet}\,\iota_0(z)}$ for all scalar $z\in\Gamma_t$ with $\theta_t z=1$; the compatibility $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ followed by $\pi_\ell$ equals $\Theta_f$; and the compatibility $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x,1)$ followed by $p_\ell$ equals $\Theta_T(\ell)x$.
--
--   Fifth, compatibility with the first degeneracy map: for all $\ell$, $B$ and $x$, the point $\Theta_T(\ell)x$ followed by $d_0(\ell)$ equals $\Theta_f(x,1)$ followed by $p$.
--
--   Sixth, compatibility with the second degeneracy map: for all $\ell$, $B$ and $x,x'$ such that $x$ is obtained from $x'$ by the twisted action of $\iota_0(s_\ell)$, the point $\Theta_T(\ell)x$ followed by $d_1(\ell)$ equals $\Theta_f(x',1)$ followed by $p$.
--
--   This is the Čerednik–Drinfeld $p$-adic uniformisation step for the Hecke tower over the Shimura curve of level $N$: from a uniformisation at full level $n$ it produces, for every prime $\ell$ away from the two ramified primes $r,\bar r$, a uniformisation of the curve with extra level $\ell$, with the two degeneracy maps realised by the identity and by the Hecke element $s_\ell$ of reduced norm $\ell$, and with a lift to any fine moduli space carrying full level $n$ and extra level $\ell$. It is used in the assembly of the full uniformisation statement including the Atkin–Lehner involutions, which in turn feeds the level-lowering input to the Frey–Serre–Ribet argument.
